# Lab 0.1: Environment Setup (Auth-Token Track)

**Course:** Cloud Computing Security Essentials  
**Lab:** 0.1 LocalStack with Auth Token + Web Console  
**Date:** 29 July 2026

---

## Objective

Activate LocalStack Pro using a license authentication token, verify license activation, configure AWS CLI endpoint access, and manage simulated AWS cloud resources (S3, DynamoDB, IAM) via both CLI commands and the web-based LocalStack Resource Browser.

---

## Part 1 & 2: Account Authentication & Terminal Token Configuration

A LocalStack account was created and a Pro Developer Auth Token was retrieved from the LocalStack Auth Tokens page. The token was securely loaded into the active Ubuntu (WSL2) shell environment using:

```bash
export LOCALSTACK_AUTH_TOKEN="ls-XXXXXXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX"
echo $LOCALSTACK_AUTH_TOKEN

Part 3: Starting the Activated LocalStack Container

Existing LocalStack instances were purged to ensure a clean slate, and a new activated LocalStack Docker container was launched with the authentication token injected via environment variables:

# 1. Remove existing containers
docker rm -f localstack 2>/dev/null

# 2. Launch LocalStack with auth token
docker run -d --name localstack \
  -p 4566:4566 \
  -p 4510-4559:4510-4559 \
  -e LOCALSTACK_AUTH_TOKEN=$LOCALSTACK_AUTH_TOKEN \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
  localstack/localstack

# 3. Watch logs until initialization is Ready
docker logs -f localstack

Part 4: License Activation & Service Health Verification

To confirm that LocalStack successfully activated the Pro license token and that core cloud services booted up properly, the info and health endpoints were checked:

curl http://localhost:4566/_localstack/info
curl http://localhost:4566/_localstack/health

Part 5: Pointing the AWS CLI at LocalStack

Dummy AWS credentials were configured in the Linux environment, and a local endpoint shortcut (EP) was created to route all CLI commands to the running LocalStack container:

aws configure set aws_access_key_id test
aws configure set aws_secret_access_key test
aws configure set region us-east-1

# Set shortcut variable
EP='--endpoint-url=http://localhost:4566'

# Test local identity connection
aws $EP sts get-caller-identity

Output Verification: Successfully returned the simulated LocalStack root identity under account ID 000000000000.

![AWS CLI Caller Identity Verification]([DRAG AND DROP SCREENSHOT OF GET-CALLER-IDENTITY HERE])

Part 6 & 7: Resource Creation & Web Console Integration

Simulated AWS cloud resources (an S3 bucket with a file, a DynamoDB table, and an IAM user) were created using the AWS CLI:

# 1. S3 Bucket & File Upload
aws $EP s3 mb s3://ccse-demo-bucket
echo "hello cloud" > hello.txt
aws $EP s3 cp hello.txt s3://ccse-demo-bucket/

# 2. DynamoDB Table
aws $EP dynamodb create-table \
  --table-name Students \
  --attribute-definitions AttributeName=id,AttributeType=S \
  --key-schema AttributeName=id,KeyType=HASH \
  --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST

# 3. IAM User
aws $EP iam create-user --user-name lab-student

Web Console Evidence:
After logging into the LocalStack Resource Browser (localhost.localstack.cloud:4566) and setting the region to us-east-1, all created resources (ccse-demo-bucket, Students table, and lab-student user) were successfully verified live in the web console.

![LocalStack Web Console Resource Browser]([DRAG AND DROP SCREENSHOT OF WEB CONSOLE RESOURCES HERE])

Part 8: Stop & Clean Up Procedures
Once the verification loop was completed, the LocalStack environment was cleaned up using standard container commands:
# Stop container (retains resources)
docker stop localstack

# Full removal (clears all simulated resources)
docker rm -f localstack

Pre-Lab Verification Checklist Completed
[x] docker --version and docker run hello-world both work.

[x] LOCALSTACK_AUTH_TOKEN is set (echo prints it).

[x] curl .../_localstack/info shows is_license_activated: true.

[x] curl .../_localstack/health responds with services available.

[x] aws $EP sts get-caller-identity returns an identity (000000000000).

[x] Resource Browser shows the default instance and test resources in us-east-1.

Conclusion
LocalStack Pro was successfully initialized and authenticated using a Pro Developer Auth Token. All required AWS CLI commands were routed to the local container endpoint, and the created cloud resources were visually confirmed via the LocalStack Resource Browser.
